const tfCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Tokens and embeddings', note: 'Text is split into tokens (~sub-words); each token becomes a vector of numbers — an embedding where similar meanings sit close together. Language becomes geometry.' },
    { math: '2 · Attention: words consult words', note: 'The transformer\'s core (2017, "Attention Is All You Need"): every token can look at every other token and pull in what\'s relevant. "It" attends back to "the trophy" — disambiguation without reciting the sentence word by word.' },
    { math: '3 · Pretraining: predict the next token', note: 'Train on trillions of tokens of internet text with one task: guess the next token. To do it well, the model must absorb grammar, facts, style and reasoning patterns — knowledge as a side effect of prediction.' },
    { math: '4 · Fine-tuning and alignment', note: 'After pretraining, instruction fine-tuning and human-feedback training (RLHF) shape the raw predictor into an assistant that follows instructions and refuses harmful requests.' },
    { math: '5 · Scale laws made it inevitable', note: 'Loss falls predictably as model size, data and compute grow (Kaplan 2020, Chinchilla 2022). Predictable improvements at unpredictable-feeling capability jumps — the engine of the GPT series and its rivals.' },
  ],
};

const tfQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'In self-attention, the weight given to each context token is computed by…', choices: ['a fixed lookup table', 'a softmax over dot products of a query with each key', 'counting word frequencies', 'the position index alone'], answer: 1, why: 'Each token\'s query is dot-producted with every token\'s key; softmax turns those scores into weights summing to 1, and the outputs are a weighted average of the value vectors. Relevance is computed, not hard-coded.' },
    { q: 'Positional encodings exist because…', choices: ['words must be numbered for the index', 'attention alone is order-blind — without them "dog bites man" and "man bites dog" are identical', 'tokens need unique IDs', 'they speed up tokenisation'], answer: 1, why: 'Self-attention treats its input as a set: every token sees every other token with no built-in order. Positional information (added to embeddings) is what lets the model distinguish word order.' },
    { q: 'Why can dot-product attention weight one token at 99.9% and the rest almost zero?', choices: ['softmax is exponential — small score differences become enormous weight differences', 'weights are rounded', 'one token is always correct', 'they cannot'], answer: 0, why: 'Scores of 8, 2 and 1 become softmax weights of about 0.997, 0.002 and 0.0009 — exponentiation sharpens small gaps into near-winner-take-all focus, which is what makes attention sharply selective.' },
    { q: 'Chinchilla\'s result (2022) said compute-optimal training means…', choices: ['making models as large as possible', 'roughly 20 training tokens per parameter — many earlier models were badly undertrained', 'training only on code', 'using fewer parameters always'], answer: 1, why: 'Hoffmann et al. showed model size and data should scale together (~20 tokens per parameter), so e.g. a 70B model wants ~1.4T tokens — explaining why smaller, longer-trained models outperformed larger, undertrained ones.' },
  ],
};

