---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/advanced/transformers-and-large-language-models">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Artificial Intelligence, and it is the ladder's summit
    for language: it presumes the foundational lesson <em>How Machines Learn</em> and the
    intermediate lessons <em>Neural Networks from Scratch</em>, <em>Classification and
    Regression</em> and <em>Image Recognition: CNNs</em>, where networks learned from
    labelled examples. Here the labels disappear — text supervises itself — and one
    architecture ends up doing translation, code and conversation at once.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair the arithmetic with its meaning" widget="match" config={tflGeometryMatch} />
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain tokens and embeddings, and connect a model's famous failures to its tokenisation;</li>
    <li>compute a softmax attention weight by hand and say what queries, keys and values do;</li>
    <li>explain why next-token prediction yields knowledge, and what instruction tuning and RLHF add;</li>
    <li>use the 20-tokens-per-parameter rule to reason about model sizing;</li>
    <li>state honestly what LLMs can and cannot do — hallucination, context windows, tool use.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate tiers gave you neural networks as stacked function approximators and NLP
    as a set of tasks — sentiment, translation, summarisation. The transformer, introduced in
    2017 in the eight-author paper "Attention Is All You Need", replaced the task-specific
    architectures of the 2010s with a single design that scales: <strong>a network whose only
    way of mixing information between positions is attention</strong>, the learned ability of
    every token to consult every other token. Trained on internet-scale text to do one thing —
    predict the next token — these models become large language models (LLMs) that write,
    translate, code and reason in ways nobody individually programmed. Understanding them is
    now the price of admission to the deep-learning-for-language syllabus (unit 24), and the
    pipeline in the widget is the honest map: tokens become vectors, attention mixes them,
    next-token prediction teaches them, alignment shapes them, and scaling laws predicted the
    whole escalation.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the LLM pipeline, stage by stage" widget="order" config={tflPipelineOrder} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="the LLM pipeline: tokens → attention → pretraining → alignment → scale" widget="equationStep" config={tfCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are the honest pipeline — geometry
    first, then the mixing mechanism, then the single training objective, then the
    alignment overlay, then the scaling law that made the escalation predictable. Keep the
    order in mind: each stage operates on the output of the one before, which is why a
    compromise made at tokenisation propagates all the way up.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one architecture, trained one way, replaced the
    task-specific designs of the 2010s. The next sections walk the pipeline in order,
    starting where every text enters it: tokens.
  </p>

  <h2>Tokens and embeddings: language as geometry</h2>
  <p>
    Neural networks eat numbers, so text is first chopped into <strong>tokens</strong> —
    sub-word units learned from data (common words stay whole; rare ones split: "unbelievable"
    may become "un", "believ", "able" — roughly 4 characters or ¾ of a word per token in
    English). Each token is looked up in a learned table of <strong>embeddings</strong>:
    vectors of a few thousand dimensions. The decisive property, discovered in the word2vec
    era (Mikolov, 2013), is that <em>directions in this space carry meaning</em>: the classic
    vector arithmetic king − man + woman lands closest to queen, and "Paris" sits near "France"
    the way "Tokyo" sits near "Japan". These are not programmed; they are the shadow that
    predicting-words-from-context casts onto geometry — words used interchangeably end up close
    together because they appear in interchangeable company. Language has become points in a
    high-dimensional space, and every later operation is geometry on those points.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the model reads text the way you do — words, or at
    least letters. It reads tokens, and the mismatch explains a whole genre of famous
    failures: counting the letters in a long word (the model sees chunks, not
    characters), arithmetic errors on large numbers (digits split across token
    boundaries inconsistently), and wordplay that lands flat. Tokenisation is an
    engineering compromise — vocabulary size against sequence length — and its seams
    show in behaviour whenever a task gets down to the character level.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — how the model reads" widget="spot-error" config={tflTokensSpot} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> text has become geometry — tokens looked up as vectors whose
    directions carry meaning. The next section adds the mechanism that made the
    transformer famous: how tokens consult one another.
  </p>

  <h2>Attention: the mechanism, with numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Attention solves a problem earlier architectures handled badly: a word's meaning depends on
    distant context, and information had to crawl through the network one step at a time. In a
    transformer, every token broadcasts three learned vectors: a <strong>query</strong> ("what
    am I looking for?"), a <strong>key</strong> ("what do I offer?") and a
    <strong>value</strong> ("what I'll contribute if selected"). Token i's attention over token
    j is a dot product of i's query with j's key — a relevance score — then all scores are
    squashed through softmax into weights that sum to 1, and i's new representation is the
    weighted average of everyone's values.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example. In "The animal didn't cross the street because it was too tired", the
    token "it" issues a query; suppose its dot products with the keys of "animal", "street"
    and "tired" come out as 8, 2 and 1. Softmax exponentiates: e⁸ ≈ 2981, e² ≈ 7.4, e¹ ≈ 2.7,
    and normalising gives weights ≈ 0.997, 0.002 and 0.0009. "It" therefore pulls almost
    entirely from "animal" — disambiguation by computation, and the exponential in softmax is
    exactly what turns a modest score gap into near-winner-take-all focus. In practice this
    happens in parallel across many <strong>attention heads</strong> (some learn to track
    syntax, some coreference, some position patterns), and each layer refines all tokens
    simultaneously. Because there is no recurrence, the model needs <strong>positional
    encodings</strong> added to the embeddings — without them, "dog bites man" and "man bites
    dog" are the same bag of tokens, since attention alone is order-blind.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full transformer block is attention plus a small <strong>feed-forward network</strong>
    applied to each position (where much of the "knowledge" is stored), with residual
    connections and layer normalisation keeping deep stacks trainable; dozens of blocks stack
    into GPT-class models. The arithmetic cost is the quadratic term: attention compares every
    token with every other, so doubling the context quadruples that part of the compute — the
    reason long-context models are an engineering frontier, not a free lunch.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="which curve is attention's bill?" widget="trace" config={tflQuadraticTrace} />
  <p>
    Generation is where the arithmetic bill arrives. An LLM produces text
    <strong>autoregressively</strong> — one token per forward pass, each new token
    conditioned on all the previous ones — so a 1,000-token answer costs 1,000
    sequential passes no matter how fast each one is. The standard optimisation is the
    <strong>KV cache</strong>: previous tokens' keys and values never change, so they
    are stored and reused instead of recomputed, trading memory for speed. This is why
    serving models is an engineering discipline of its own: prompt processing runs
    across thousands of tokens in parallel, but token generation is inherently serial,
    and moving the cached keys and values is usually the binding constraint — memory
    bandwidth, not matrix multiplication.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> attention "understands" or "highlights important words" the
    way a highlighter does. Attention is a routing computation — it decides which vectors get
    mixed into which — and it is only one part of the stack. Interpreting individual attention
    weights as explanations has repeatedly failed (the same output can arise from different
    attention patterns), which is why model explanation is its own research field.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — reading the attention weights" widget="judge" config={tflAttentionJudge} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> attention routes information between positions, softmax makes
    it sharply selective, and positional encodings restore the order attention alone
    cannot see. Next comes the training objective that turns this machinery into
    something that knows things.
  </p>

  <h2>Pretraining: knowledge as a side effect of prediction</h2>
  <p>
    Training has one objective: given a prefix of tokens, predict the next one, scored by
    cross-entropy loss and corrected by gradient descent — trillions of times. The
    <strong>self-supervision</strong> trick is that the labels are free: every text corpus on
    Earth is its own training set, no human annotation needed. To predict well, a model cannot
    just memorise: it must compress regularities — grammar to place plausible words, world
    knowledge to continue "the capital of Australia is", discourse structure to close a
    paragraph, reasoning patterns to continue a proof or a joke that hasn't paid off yet.
    "Learning" here is compression under a prediction deadline. What the raw predictor does
    <em>not</em> do is follow instructions — it completes text, so "How do I make bread?" might
    be completed with "…the question many bakers ask". The gap between text-completer and
    assistant is closed in post-training: <strong>instruction fine-tuning</strong> on
    curated question–answer pairs, then <strong>RLHF</strong> — humans rank model outputs, a
    reward model learns the ranking, and reinforcement learning (the sibling lesson) pushes the
    policy toward preferred behaviour. This alignment stage is a small fraction of the compute
    but most of the difference between a document-finisher and something you can delegate to.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drag the divider — completer to assistant" widget="compare" config={tflAssistantCompare} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> an LLM looks up answers in a database. It stores knowledge
    as <em>weights</em> — a lossy, statistical compression of training text — which is exactly
    why it can be fluent about things it gets subtly wrong, why quoting it is unsafe without
    verification, and why its knowledge is frozen at training time unless tools (search,
    retrieval) are bolted on.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> self-supervision made the labels free, compression under a
    prediction deadline stored knowledge in weights, and post-training turned a
    text-completer into an assistant. The final mechanism planned the whole era: what
    happens when you scale it all up.
  </p>

  <h2>Scaling laws: the engine of the era</h2>
  <p>
    The field's most consequential empirical finding (Kaplan et al., 2020) is that
    <strong>loss falls as a smooth power law</strong> in model size, dataset size and compute —
    over many orders of magnitude. Predictability at that scale is unheard of in software, and
    it converted research into logistics: if you know what doubling compute buys, you can plan
    the next model's budget. Hoffmann et al.'s <strong>Chinchilla</strong> result (2022) refined
    the economics: for a fixed compute budget, size and data should grow <em>together</em> —
    roughly 20 training tokens per parameter. A 70-billion-parameter model wants about 1.4
    trillion tokens; models trained before this rule were "undertrained", which is why
    Chinchilla's 70B beat its 280B sibling. Downstream capabilities — translation quality,
    arithmetic, code — improve unevenly on top of the smooth loss curve, which is why progress
    feels jagged even though the underlying scaling is smooth.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the magnitude — the compute-optimal corpus" widget="estimate" config={tflChinchillaEstimate} />
  <p>
    The rule is worth computing once by hand. A 7-billion-parameter model wants about
    20 × 7 = 140 billion tokens; a 70B model wants 1.4 trillion. Flip it around and the
    consequence is stark: if your corpus is 1.4 trillion tokens, the compute-optimal
    plan is roughly one 70B model — not four 7B models trained four times over, and not
    one 400B model starved of data (Chinchilla's own comparison: its 70B, trained on
    1.4T tokens, beat its 280B sibling trained on 300B). The 20:1 ratio turned model
    sizing from folklore into arithmetic.
  </p>

  <h2>What LLMs can and can't do — honestly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>They predict tokens, and that explains the quirks:</strong> hallucination (plausible-sounding falsehoods are just good predictions), sensitivity to phrasing, and knowledge frozen at training time (unless retrieval is added).</li>
    <li><strong>Context windows are working memory:</strong> models attend within a bounded window (128K+ tokens now) — anything outside doesn't exist. Long tasks are workflows, not one prompt.</li>
    <li><strong>Emergent abilities are disputed:</strong> some skills appear only at scale; researchers debate whether they're true capability jumps or measurement artefacts. The benchmark question is active research.</li>
    <li><strong>Tool use extends them:</strong> calling search, code execution and APIs turns the model into an orchestrator — agentic systems. Reliability, cost and safety of those loops are the current engineering frontier (the alignment lesson continues the story).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: why do positional encodings exist, and what exactly goes wrong without them?</li>
    <li>Application: a query has dot-product scores 8, 2 and 1 against three keys. Without computing exact exponentials, say which token dominates the attention output and why softmax sharpens the gap.</li>
    <li>Why-reasoning: why does asking an LLM to count the letters in a long word trip it up, and what does that reveal about where its knowledge lives?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Self-attention treats its input as a set — every token
    sees every other with no built-in order — so "dog bites man" and "man bites dog" would
    be identical without positional information added to the embeddings. (2)
    Exponentiation magnifies score differences: 8, 2 and 1 become weights of roughly
    0.997, 0.002 and 0.0009, so the output is a near-winner-take-all mix pulled almost
    entirely from the first token — selectivity by computation, not by rule. (3) Because
    the model reads chunks, not characters: tokenisation splits words into sub-word
    units, so the individual characters are not present in its input. The same mechanism
    explains arithmetic slips on large numbers, and it shows that the model's knowledge
    lives in weights as a lossy statistical compression — not in a lookup table of
    letters or facts.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The transformer's single mixing mechanism is attention: every token computes relevance to every other via query–key dot products.</li>
    <li>Softmax's exponential turns modest score gaps into near-winner-take-all focus; positional encodings restore the order attention is blind to.</li>
    <li>Pretraining predicts the next token on trillions of tokens; knowledge is a side effect of compression under a prediction deadline.</li>
    <li>Instruction tuning and RLHF close the gap between text-completer and assistant — a small fraction of the compute, most of the difference.</li>
    <li>Scaling laws made loss predictable: roughly 20 tokens per parameter is the compute-optimal ratio.</li>
    <li>Generation is autoregressive and serial; the KV cache trades memory for speed and shifts the bottleneck to bandwidth.</li>
    <li>Hallucination, frozen knowledge and tokenisation seams all trace to one fact: the model stores lossy statistical weights, not looked-up facts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling advanced lessons complete the picture: <em>Reinforcement Learning</em>
    explains the RLHF stage this chapter compressed into a paragraph, and
    <em>Evaluation, Bias and Safety</em> interrogates exactly the benchmark scores and
    failure modes this chapter's models produce. For what comes after — interpretability,
    alignment, generative models beyond text — the expert tier continues the ladder.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why attention won: parallelism.</strong> Recurrent networks (LSTMs) process tokens
    sequentially, so training cannot parallelise across the sequence and long-range information
    decays through the steps. Attention touches every pair of positions in one matrix
    multiplication, so a GPU crunches thousands of tokens at once — the transformer is the
    architecture the hardware wanted. The quadratic cost is the price; the research frontier is
    cheaper approximations (sparse attention, linear-attention and state-space models like
    Mamba) that keep the scaling while shedding the n² term. Whether any of them displaces
    vanilla attention at frontier scale is genuinely open.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="design the successor — three architectures, three trade-offs" widget="branch" config={tflSuccessorBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>In-context learning and what it means.</strong> Large models exhibit a strange
    capability: shown a few solved examples in the prompt ("few-shot prompting"), they continue
    the pattern without any weight update — the context itself steers the function. Theoretical
    work (induction heads, implicit gradient-like updates) suggests the transformer is running
    small learning algorithms <em>inside</em> its forward pass. Meanwhile chain-of-thought
    prompting — asking the model to reason step by step before answering — measurably improves
    multi-step tasks, and training explicitly on long reasoning traces ("reasoning models")
    pushes the same lever harder. Whether the resulting step-by-step text is computation or
    performance of computation is a live and slightly uncomfortable question.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open problems.</strong> Mechanistic interpretability (reverse-engineering circuits
    inside the weights) has found real structures — induction heads, factual-association
    pathways — but explains a sliver of frontier models. Hallucination resists simple fixes
    because the objective rewards plausible continuation, not verified truth (some researchers
    argue calibrated uncertainty is the honest goal). Data is becoming the binding constraint:
    high-quality human text is finite, so synthetic data, curated corpora and legal provenance
    now shape frontier training. And evaluation has not caught up — training-data contamination
    means benchmark scores overstate capability, pushing the field toward live, private and
    agentic evaluations (the sibling lesson on evaluation, bias and safety takes this up).
  </p>

